    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (there's only one test class; the other two files are support/base classes). \n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**\n- Updated the call site to pass the city: `nextCustomerCode(owner.getCity(), owner.getLastName())`.\n- Rewrote `nextCustomerCode` to build `CITY3` (upper-cased first three letters of city) and `LAST3` (first three of lastName), and compute `NNNN` as a **per-city** sequence \u2014 one more than the count of existing owners in that same city (matched case-insensitively via the existing `normalizeIdentity` helper, consistent with the codebase's other comparisons).\n- Extracted a shared `letterPrefix3` helper (used for both city and last name) that upper-cases the first three letters, skipping non-letter characters.\n\n**`Owner.java`**\n- The membership number is `<customerCode>-M<YY>` \u2014 since it embeds `customerCode` directly, it automatically rebuilds on the new format (e.g. `LON-SMI-0007-M26`). Updated its javadoc example accordingly.\n\n**`openapi.yml`**\n- Updated the `customerCode` and `membershipNumber` descriptions and examples to reflect the new city-prefixed format.\n\nThe acceptance test (`customerCode` matches `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}`) and the full build pass. The `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` columns comfortably fit the longer values.",
